                              PACKFIDO.EXE
                      pcbirc crew, 2026.  GPLv3.
                 The 15.4 upgrade that was never shipped

              pcb153\upd154\SOURCE\MISC\PACKFIDO


This utility packs the FIDO area file AREAS.DAT, the format PCBoard has
used since 15.22.


WHY IT IS FILED IN upd154
-------------------------

upd154 is Clark's 15.4 upgrade to 15.3 in source form: 15.3 is the base,
upd154 is the delta, which is the same shape the 15.4 binary patches
had.  A PACKFIDO that reads the current data structure IS a 15.4 upgrade
to a 15.3 board, so it belongs in the delta.  pcb153\SOURCE stays pure.

And 15.3 is where the evidence that it was needed lives - the shipped
PACKFIDO.EXE, DOC\PACK.DOC calling it a 15.21 tool, and CONVERT.CPP with
the pack step commented out.  The problem is documented in the base; the
fix is filed in the upgrade.


WHY IT EXISTS
-------------

Clark shipped a PACKFIDO.EXE in the 15.41 install set.  Its own
documentation, DOC\PACK.DOC, copyright 1995, describes a utility
"written for PCBoard 15.21 to pack the FIDO configuration file
PCBFIDO.CFG."  That is exactly what it does, and PCBFIDO.CFG stopped
holding the areas at 15.22.

It was never updated.  Every binary in that install set that touches
current data carries a 15.3 version string - PCBOARD, PCBOARDM, PCBSM,
PCBPACK, PCBSETUP, MKPCBTXT, UUIN, UUOUT, UUXFER, PCBMODEM, PPLC330.
PACKFIDO.EXE carries 15.0 and nothing later.  FIDOUTIL.EXE, the program
that CONVERTS 15.21 data to 15.22, carries 15.0, 15.21 and 15.22 - it
knew about the new format.  The program whose whole job is packing the
file FIDOUTIL had just converted never learned it.

The converter's own source says the same thing a third way.  In
MISC\FIDOUTIL\SOURCE\CONVERT.CPP, the call to packfido's one exported
symbol is commented out:

    //do_pack();

The pack step was disabled at conversion time rather than fixed.

So a 15.3 or 15.4 sysop had a PACKFIDO.EXE on disk, documented as a
15.21 tool, that could not read the file it was pointed at.  This is the
successor that was never written.


USING IT
--------

    PACKFIDO <areas.dat> <cnames> <numconf> [/R]

      <areas.dat>  the FIDO area file, version 3
      <cnames>     the CNAMES base name, with or without an extension;
                   both CNAMES.@@@ and CNAMES.ADD are read
      <numconf>    number of conferences
      /R           report only - read and decide, write nothing

Make a backup copy of AREAS.DAT first.  As records are packed out of the
file it will be noted on the screen, in the same form the original used:

    removed     5 4DOS...

Clark's version took no arguments; it read PCBOARD.DAT from the current
directory and found everything from there.  This one takes them on the
command line on purpose.  A utility you can point at a copy of a file is
a utility you can test, and this one has no shipped binary to be
compared against - see NO ORACLE below.


WHAT GETS REMOVED
-----------------

An area record is dropped for three reasons, the same three the original
used:

    1. its conference number is above the conference count
    2. its conference is not a FIDO conference
    3. an area for that conference was already kept

A conference is a FIDO conference when it has a name, has a message
base, and has ConfType 5.  The name and the message base come from
CNAMES.@@@; ConfType comes from CNAMES.ADD.  A board with no CNAMES.ADD
has no FIDO conferences at all, which is correct - ConfType did not
exist before that file.

Reason 3 is duplicate removal, and it is half of what PACKFIDO is for.
The FIRST area for a conference is kept and every later one is removed.


THE RECORD SIZE IS MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED
----------------------------------------

Three sources disagree about the size of an AREAS.DAT record:

    STRUCTS.H, sizeof(NAREA_STRUCT)                      81
    FIDO.DOC, the record laid out over offsets 2..82     81
    Clark's own AREAS.DAT                                71

    48,566 - 2 = 48,564 ;  48,564 / 71 = 684.0000 exactly

The ten bytes are the trailing reserved[10].  It is in the header and in
the document; it is not in the data.  So the size is probed: 81 first,
then 71, and the first one that divides the file evenly AND whose every
record has a sane conference number and a printable area tag is the one
used.  It is printed at the start of the run.

The pack loop reads raw bytes rather than a structure, so any byte it
does not understand - including a reserved[10] that may or may not be
there - is copied through untouched instead of overwritten.


NO ORACLE
---------

No shipped binary ever packed version 3, so there is nothing to compare
this against byte for byte.  It is checked against Clark's own
AREAS.DAT, kept in pcb153\SOURCE\MISC\FIDOUTIL\: 684 records at
71 bytes, 683 kept, the one non-FIDO conference removed, and /R leaves
the file untouched.

That is a weaker check than a byte diff, and it is said plainly here
rather than dressed up.  The 15.3 rebuild in pcb153\SOURCE\MISC\PACKFIDO
is the one with a real oracle, because Clark's binary exists to compare
it to.
